# D405 serial → arm side map for nero-detect-pika.
#
# This file gets installed to /etc/nero/d405_sides.conf by
# setup/install_pika_detector.sh on first run. Once the per-host file
# exists, the installer leaves it alone — edit it in place when the
# hardware changes; don't commit machine-specific serials back here.
#
# Format: one assignment per line, "<side> <serial>". Comments and
# blank lines are ignored. A side may appear more than once — useful
# because the D405 firmware can flip between reporting its device
# serial and its ASIC serial on reconnect, so listing every serial
# we've ever seen for that physical camera lets the detector and
# webapp try each in turn until one opens.
#
# --- identifying a D405's side ---------------------------------------
# Plug only one arm's Pika bundle in. Observe which serial(s) appear:
#   for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do \
#     python3 -c "import pyrealsense2 as rs; \
#       [print(d.get_info(rs.camera_info.serial_number)) \
#        for d in rs.context().devices]" | sort -u; \
#     sleep 1; \
#   done | sort -u
# Label whatever shows up with the arm that's plugged in. Add a line
# per distinct serial observed (multiple lines per side are fine).
# Then plug the other arm in and repeat.
#
# --- current hardware snapshot (CloudChef Jetson feather) ------------
# Leave these commented out in the repo; uncomment in the /etc copy on
# the target host. The installer will drop them verbatim into
# /etc/nero/d405_sides.conf the first time it runs, then never touch
# the file again.

# right 412622273124
# right 243223071734
# left  230322274240
